{"metadata": {"team_id": "ucsc", "run_id": "UCSC-SIMRAG-ensemble", "run_type": "automatic"}, "turn_id": "1-1_1", "responses": [{"rank": 1, "text": "For acid reflux, focusing on low-acid, high-fiber, and high-water content foods can be beneficial.\n\nExcellent choices include:\n*   **Fruits:** Bananas, melons (like watermelon, cantaloupe, honeydew), and apples are highly alkaline and can help coat the esophageal lining. Other non-acidic fruits such as guava and papaya are also good.\n*   **Vegetables:** Green vegetables like asparagus, Brussels sprouts, kale, spinach, broccoli, green beans, peas, cucumbers, and carrots are low in fat and alkaline. Potatoes are also a good option.\n*   **Grains:** Oatmeal is high in fiber, absorbs stomach acid, and promotes healthy digestion. Whole-grain breads, brown rice, and couscous are also recommended.\n*   **Lean Proteins:** Skinless chicken, turkey, fish (especially oily fish like salmon for healthy fats), and lean cuts of pork or beef are easier to digest. Egg whites are preferred over yolks.\n*   **Other helpful foods:** Almonds and other nuts, unsweetened coconut water, ginger (which has anti-inflammatory properties), and fat-free dairy products like skim milk or fat-free yogurt can also provide relief. Unsweetened, natural, smooth peanut butter and honey may also be tolerated.\n\nRemember that overeating can trigger symptoms, so eating filling foods in moderation is key. It's generally advised to avoid high-fat foods, citrus fruits, spicy foods, garlic, and onions, as these can worsen reflux.", "citations": {"clueweb22-en0042-37-07571:2": 4.9309349293618485, "clueweb22-en0015-18-13111:4": 4.862202578156347, "clueweb22-en0005-41-09262:15": 4.801962362151191, "clueweb22-en0039-65-00093:3": 4.739269108026406, "clueweb22-en0005-41-09262:16": 4.653493543651701, "clueweb22-en0009-09-00824:3": 4.649397359834114, "clueweb22-en0009-09-00824:2": 4.641591711730211, "clueweb22-en0002-93-16983:2": 4.633775880145951, "clueweb22-en0021-4