{"metadata": {"team_id": "ucsc", "run_id": "UCSC-SIMRAG-ensemble", "run_type": "automatic"}, "turn_id": "1-1_4", "responses": [{"rank": 1, "text": "To manage acid reflux, it's generally recommended to avoid foods and drinks that can irritate the esophagus or increase stomach acid. Based on the information, here's a list of items to consider limiting or avoiding:\n\n*   **High-fat and Fried Foods:** This includes items like french fries, onion rings, fatty cuts of meat (bacon, sausage, red meats), full-fat dairy products (whole milk, butter, cheese, sour cream, ice cream), creamy sauces, and processed snacks like potato chips. These can relax the lower esophageal sphincter (LES) and delay stomach emptying.\n*   **Acidic Fruits and Vegetables:** Steer clear of citrus fruits (lemons, oranges, grapefruits, limes, pineapples) and their juices. Tomatoes, along with tomato-based products like sauce, ketchup, and soup, are also highly acidic. As discussed previously, pomegranates are also best avoided due to their acidity.\n*   **Spicy Foods and Certain Aromatics:** Foods with hot spices (chili, pepper flakes, hot peppers) can irritate the esophagus. Onions and garlic are also common triggers.\n*   **Mint:** Peppermint and spearmint, often found in gum or tea, can relax the LES and worsen reflux.\n*   **Beverages:** Carbonated drinks (sodas), coffee, most caffeinated teas, and alcohol are known to trigger symptoms.\n*   **Other Triggers:** Chocolate, sugary foods (like candy), and fast food are frequently reported as problematic. Eating large meals, especially late at night, should also be avoided.", "citations": {"clueweb22-en0034-09-00524:2": 4.8562369954953954, "clueweb22-en0013-66-13847:15": 4.800937267802722, "clueweb22-en0037-98-07152:4": 4.710716947550823, "clueweb22-en0004-59-18085:9": 4.680997729237641, "clueweb22-en0018-87-10874:12": 4.646855673506385, "clueweb22-en0013-66-13847:14": 4.601881235109101, "clueweb22-en0018-50-11252:14": 4.600040712122913, "clueweb22-