{"metadata": {"team_id": "ucsc", "run_id": "UCSC-SIMRAG-ensemble", "run_type": "automatic"}, "turn_id": "1-1_6", "responses": [{"rank": 1, "text": "Yes, apple cider vinegar (ACV) is indeed acidic. This is why its recommendation for acid reflux can seem counter-intuitive.\n\nThe theory behind using ACV is that some cases of acid reflux are caused by *too little* stomach acid, not too much. When stomach acid levels are low, the lower esophageal sphincter (LES) may not close properly, allowing existing acid to reflux. Proponents suggest that introducing a small amount of ACV (diluted in water) can help increase stomach acidity, aiding digestion and signaling the LES to close more effectively.\n\nHowever, many experts and studies contradict this. They argue that ACV, being acidic, can actually worsen acid reflux symptoms, especially for conditions like silent reflux (LPR), by further irritating the esophagus and activating digestive enzymes outside the stomach. There is also a lack of strong scientific evidence to consistently support ACV as an effective treatment for acid reflux. Some sources even warn that regular intake could contribute to esophageal damage or erode tooth enamel.\n\nGiven these conflicting views and potential risks, it's best to consult a healthcare professional before using apple cider vinegar for acid reflux.", "citations": {"clueweb22-en0007-10-18426:18": 4.8026246875873655, "clueweb22-en0027-96-01816:0": 4.520258907534485, "clueweb22-en0032-91-00840:17": 4.390970550884907, "clueweb22-en0042-89-04330:9": 4.231511017492493, "clueweb22-en0025-73-02622:9": 4.196635097879903, "clueweb22-en0030-73-19245:2": 4.175271176323548, "clueweb22-en0002-87-15707:6": 4.02735856843396, "clueweb22-en0030-73-19245:5": 3.955451928847275}, "ptkb_provenance": []}], "references": {"clueweb22-en0007-10-18426:18": 4.8026246875873655, "clueweb22-en0038-17-12281:10": 4.620747190970865, "clueweb22-en0027-96-01816:0": 4.520258907534485, "clueweb22-en0038-17-12281:9": 4.518685