{"metadata": {"team_id": "ucsc", "run_id": "UCSC-SIMRAG-ensemble", "run_type": "automatic"}, "turn_id": "1-1_10", "responses": [{"rank": 1, "text": "H2 blockers and Proton Pump Inhibitors (PPIs) both work to reduce stomach acid, but they do so through different mechanisms.\n\nH2 blockers (Histamine-2 blockers) work by blocking histamine receptors in the stomach lining. Histamine is a substance that stimulates the stomach to produce gastric acid. By blocking these receptors, H2 blockers reduce the levels of histamine that can agitate acid-creating cells, thereby decreasing the overall production of stomach acid.\n\nProton Pump Inhibitors (PPIs) are a class of medications that target the \"proton pump\" (specifically, the hydrogen-potassium adenosine triphosphatase enzyme system) in the cells lining the stomach. This pump is responsible for the final step in producing stomach acid. PPIs work by inhibiting or shutting down these pumps, significantly reducing the amount of acid the stomach produces.", "citations": {"clueweb22-en0034-18-07329:2": 4.9315684710917775, "clueweb22-en0029-78-09970:5": 4.882193727312153, "clueweb22-en0012-22-15649:4": 4.705107087052942, "clueweb22-en0043-49-02220:6": 4.701264898975349, "clueweb22-en0029-78-09970:4": 4.6448158116434435, "clueweb22-en0018-78-05578:4": 4.639355326470588}, "ptkb_provenance": []}], "references": {"clueweb22-en0034-18-07329:2": 4.9315684710917775, "clueweb22-en0032-45-03257:16": 4.922168547544289, "clueweb22-en0004-00-09704:7": 4.916565804673623, "clueweb22-en0029-78-09970:5": 4.882193727312153, "clueweb22-en0016-53-08494:9": 4.793515891376816, "clueweb22-en0029-78-09970:6": 4.780068077844613, "clueweb22-en0046-25-11674:5": 4.764981341328618, "clueweb22-en0016-53-08494:10": 4.7447663107309515, "clueweb22-en0040-98-04604:3": 4.730816823264098, "clueweb22-en0018-00-12905:2": 4.709144470873717, "clueweb22-en0012-22-15649:4": 4.705107087052942, "clueweb22-en0041-89-18148:7": 4.701635379061856, "clueweb22-en0043-49-02220