{"metadata": {"team_id": "ucsc", "run_id": "UCSC-SIMRAG-ensemble", "run_type": "automatic"}, "turn_id": "1-2_3", "responses": [{"rank": 1, "text": "Here are some practical psychological tips to help you with your digital well-being:\n\n*   **Set SMART Goals:** Define specific, measurable, attainable, relevant, and time-bound goals for your digital media use. For example, instead of \"I will cut down my digital media use,\" try \"I will spend no more than one hour watching Netflix on weekdays.\"\n*   **Practice Self-Reflection:** Increase your self-awareness about your digital usage patterns. Identify which applications you use repetitively and recognize the emotional or psychological triggers that prompt this excessive consumption. Reflect on thoughts that lead to increased screen time.\n*   **Find Alternatives:** Once you identify triggers, define alternative behaviors or interests to satisfy those needs. For instance, if stress leads to doom scrolling, try mindfulness meditation or other stress-relieving activities instead.\n*   **Intentional Disengagement (Digital Detox):** Consciously limit your technology use and take breaks. This can involve putting down your devices to read, go outside, visit loved ones, or engage in creative activities. Avoid compulsive and mindless scrolling by being intentional with your online time.\n*   **Prioritize Other Activities:** Ensure your digital use doesn't detract from physical activity, sufficient sleep, or in-person interactions with friends and family. Consider keeping devices out of your bedroom.\n*   **Manage Notifications and Content:** Turn off unnecessary notifications and be mindful of the quality and content you consume online. Focus on active use, like creating or connecting, rather than passive scrolling.\n*   **Seek Support:** If you're struggling, consider online peer support groups or discuss your concerns with friends, a GP, or a psychologist.", "citations": {"clueweb22-en0016-89-18200:4": 4.14951483808549, "cl