{"metadata": {"team_id": "ucsc", "run_id": "UCSC-SIMRAG-ensemble", "run_type": "automatic"}, "turn_id": "2-1_2", "responses": [{"rank": 1, "text": "To improve the taste of your coffee and make it gentler on your stomach due to mild acid reflux, consider these adjustments:\n\nFirst, **water quality** is crucial. Using filtered water can significantly reduce the acidity of your coffee by cutting down on dissolved minerals in tap water (Passages 1, 2, 6). Some sources even suggest alkaline water for brewing if you experience acid reflux (Passage 7).\n\nSecond, **regularly clean your coffee machine**. Buildup of oils and residue can cause off-flavors and contribute to acidity. Cleaning helps neutralize leftover acidity from previous brews (Passages 1, 9, 14).\n\nThird, experiment with your **grind size and coffee-to-water ratio**. An under-extracted coffee, often due to a grind that's too coarse or too little water, can taste acidic (Passages 10, 15, 17). Adjusting your grind to be finer or using a proper coffee-to-water ratio can help achieve a balanced extraction (Passage 20).\n\nFourth, consider **adding a pinch of salt** to your coffee, as it can mellow out acidity and bitterness (Passages 3, 11). Adding milk or cream can also help to dull the acidic 'bite' (Passages 14, 16).\n\nFinally, for a significantly less acidic option, you might try **cold brew coffee**. It's brewed cold, resulting in a naturally sweeter taste and is approximately two-thirds less acidic than hot coffee, making it much better tolerated for those with acid reflux (Passages 12, 16).", "citations": {"clueweb22-en0006-16-13801:13": 4.719230859709247, "clueweb22-en0006-16-13801:14": 4.715775514663495, "clueweb22-en0039-57-02591:6": 4.532324370681694, "clueweb22-en0038-48-07837:19": 4.3126948431872165, "clueweb22-en0029-58-17697:6": 4.3112180431632074, "clueweb22-en0031-11-05936:7": 4.111488533960218, "clueweb22-en0011-67-08408:13": 4.025066582692516, "clueweb22-en0022-86-01432:11": 4.0162073218413