{"metadata": {"team_id": "ucsc", "run_id": "UCSC-SIMRAG-ensemble", "run_type": "automatic"}, "turn_id": "2-1_3", "responses": [{"rank": 1, "text": "To make your coffee taste better and be more gentle on your stomach, there are several effective methods you can try.\n\nOne popular approach is adding a small amount of **baking soda** to your coffee grounds before brewing, or a tiny pinch to your brewed cup. This helps neutralize the coffee's acidity, which can reduce acid reflux symptoms and make the taste smoother, especially if you find coffee too harsh (Passages 1, 5, 9, 13, 16).\n\nWhile you've heard about salt, adding a **pinch of salt** can also significantly reduce bitterness and may help mitigate acid reflux by raising the coffee's pH (Passages 3, 17).\n\nConsider the **type of coffee and brewing method**. Darker roasts tend to be less acidic than lighter ones (Passages 6, 10, 12). **Cold-brew coffee** is another excellent option as it naturally results in a less acidic, smoother, and often sweeter taste (Passages 6, 10, 14, 19). You might also look for coffee brands specifically marketed as low-acid, which are designed to be gentler on the stomach (Passages 5, 20).\n\nFinally, ensure your **coffee machine is clean**. Regular cleaning, for example with vinegar, removes residue that can cause a bitter or stale taste (Passages 2, 4, 7). Adjusting your grind size can also impact taste; a coarser grind can reduce bitterness from over-extraction (Passage 8).", "citations": {"clueweb22-en0024-78-17645:5": 4.544301443668845, "clueweb22-en0039-92-06362:14": 4.409356244418909, "clueweb22-en0035-41-14346:10": 4.215888778205492, "clueweb22-en0015-02-10471:6": 4.207122725201956, "clueweb22-en0045-23-13438:1": 4.206363946712672, "clueweb22-en0017-76-18714:8": 4.142823950733891, "clueweb22-en0014-98-18372:18": 4.053601313021959, "clueweb22-en0014-71-15177:8": 3.952100517116098, "clueweb22-en0007-67-02540:11": 3.8790019873998958, "clueweb22-en0026-27-09966:6": 3.874637683373