{"metadata": {"team_id": "ucsc", "run_id": "UCSC-SIMRAG-ensemble", "run_type": "automatic"}, "turn_id": "2-1_5", "responses": [{"rank": 1, "text": "Given your preference for beverages gentle on the stomach and your occasional acid reflux, exploring low-acid coffee beans is a great idea. Sourness in coffee can sometimes be linked to acidity.\n\nLow-acid coffees are specifically processed or grown to reduce their acid content, making them easier on sensitive stomachs and reducing symptoms like heartburn or acid reflux.\n\nWhen choosing, consider Arabica beans, which naturally have less caffeine and are a good choice. Coffees from regions like Mexico and Sumatra are also known for lower acidity. Darker roasts generally contain less acid than lighter roasts, as the roasting process can reduce acid levels. Beans grown at lower elevations or in the shade also tend to be naturally less acidic.\n\nBrewing methods also play a role; cold brewing, for instance, significantly reduces acidity, creating a smoother drink. Some decaf coffees, particularly those processed with the Mountain Water method, are also lower in acid.\n\nMany brands now specialize in low-acid coffees, using unique roasting processes like slow roasting, or steam and water methods to achieve this. You can find brands like Puroast, Hevla, Lucy Jo's, and Tieman's Fusion Coffee, which are designed to be stomach-friendly.", "citations": {"clueweb22-en0020-02-11390:3": 4.636856271367057, "clueweb22-en0026-27-09966:6": 4.514134759410686, "clueweb22-en0040-56-13241:0": 4.498198703048943, "clueweb22-en0026-39-04357:3": 4.3263590992666625, "clueweb22-en0024-59-04022:5": 4.279649743559771, "clueweb22-en0015-13-06066:5": 4.239029057056802, "clueweb22-en0026-27-09966:7": 4.173355176676545, "clueweb22-en0040-56-13241:2": 4.154586885440872, "clueweb22-en0040-56-13241:1": 4.149134704019, "clueweb22-en0005-28-08200:8": 4.143560852146804, "clueweb22-en0005-28-08200:9": 4.131021629114237, "clueweb22-en0045-27-00467:13": 4.1213