{"metadata": {"team_id": "ucsc", "run_id": "UCSC-SIMRAG-ensemble", "run_type": "automatic"}, "turn_id": "2-1_6", "responses": [{"rank": 1, "text": "There are many factors that influence the taste of coffee beans, from their origin to how they are processed and roasted.\n\nThe two main types of coffee beans are Arabica and Robusta. Arabica beans are generally considered higher quality, offering a smoother taste with a broad range of scents, often described as sweeter due to higher lipid and sugar content. They also have less caffeine. Robusta beans are more inexpensive, contain about twice as much caffeine, and tend to be more bitter due to higher levels of chlorogenic acid.\n\nThe region where coffee is grown significantly impacts its flavor. For instance, Central and South American coffees are often clean and sweet, Asian coffees feature earthy flavors, and African beans are complex and fruit-forward. Higher altitudes typically lead to slower bean maturation, resulting in more complex, sweeter, and acidic flavors.\n\nProcessing methods also play a role: natural (dry) processing, where the whole cherry is dried, often yields a sweeter, fruitier taste. Washed (wet) processing, where the fruit is removed before drying, results in a cleaner but more acidic flavor.\n\nFinally, the roast level dramatically changes the taste. Light roasts are often mild and balanced with more caffeine, while medium roasts offer a balanced flavor. Medium-dark roasts are more full-bodied, and dark roasts develop a bitter, smoky taste due to higher temperatures during roasting.", "citations": {"clueweb22-en0016-20-09441:8": 4.423727797354458, "clueweb22-en0022-18-11457:2": 4.099833720851477, "clueweb22-en0024-59-04022:15": 3.918371789384563, "clueweb22-en0016-20-09441:9": 3.700220770982911, "clueweb22-en0026-91-15281:8": 3.541890329082857, "clueweb22-en0016-20-09441:10": 3.440052264659906}, "ptkb_provenance": []}], "references": {"clueweb22-en0033-49-03668:5": 4.90203067266559, "clueweb22-en