{"metadata": {"team_id": "ucsc", "run_id": "UCSC-SIMRAG-ensemble", "run_type": "automatic"}, "turn_id": "2-1_11", "responses": [{"rank": 1, "text": "Roasting profoundly impacts the taste and acidity of coffee beans, transforming them from bitter and highly acidic green beans into a drinkable beverage with distinct flavors and aromas. This process involves significant physical and chemical changes, such as the caramelization of sugars and the Maillard reaction, which create new flavor compounds.\n\nGenerally, the longer and darker the roast, the less acidic the coffee becomes. Light roasts retain the most acidity, often presenting bright, floral, or fruity notes with a thinner body. As beans are roasted to a medium level, the acidity becomes more balanced, and the coffee develops a fuller, richer flavor.\n\nDarker roasts significantly reduce acidity, but they can introduce bitterness. This bitterness arises as chlorogenic acids break down into other acids and compounds like phenylindanes during intense roasting. While dark roasts offer a deep, rich taste and can have smoky or woody notes, excessive roasting can lead to a harsh, charred, or overly bitter flavor, overpowering the bean's delicate characteristics. The oils also migrate to the surface, influencing the flavor profile.", "citations": {"clueweb22-en0041-01-05927:8": 4.9971885988003315, "clueweb22-en0001-58-05338:1": 4.779611734661538, "clueweb22-en0034-86-14025:0": 4.468690761651329, "clueweb22-en0035-10-05441:8": 4.450696052345041, "clueweb22-en0040-04-13369:2": 4.445197832434949, "clueweb22-en0026-82-04612:8": 4.418862703659851, "clueweb22-en0022-32-07211:5": 4.389168321476924, "clueweb22-en0001-58-05338:11": 4.354123921831793, "clueweb22-en0005-59-07136:15": 4.334579506491897, "clueweb22-en0040-04-13369:13": 4.3223148135439375, "clueweb22-en0021-26-17141:1": 4.321679971500149, "clueweb22-en0033-61-13869:3": 4.310237188831915, "clueweb22-en0041-01-05927:11": 4.303085166900104, "clueweb22-en0041-01-05927:10