{"metadata": {"team_id": "ucsc", "run_id": "UCSC-SIMRAG-ensemble", "run_type": "automatic"}, "turn_id": "3-1_6", "responses": [{"rank": 1, "text": "Given your symptoms, it sounds like you might have a Grade 1 (sprain) or a partial Grade 2 A2 pulley tear. The first crucial step is to seek a diagnosis from a climbing-knowledgeable physician or orthopedic surgeon. They can assess the damage, often with an ultrasound or MRI, to determine the exact grade of your injury and rule out other issues.\n\nFor a Grade 1 pulley sprain, you typically don't need immobilization. Instead, you should begin gentle range-of-motion exercises\u2014bending and straightening your finger until it feels tight, holding briefly, and repeating. It's important to avoid pushing through pain.\n\nIf it's a Grade 2 tear, one to two weeks of immobilization with a finger-immobilization or pulley-protection splint may be recommended, along with gentle range-of-motion exercises. H-taping is also advised for support, especially when you gradually return to climbing, for up to three to six months.\n\nDuring recovery, avoid pushing through pain. As you heal, you can slowly build up resistance training with exercises like putty, rubber band exercises, or very light dumbbell curls. For long-term prevention, ensure you warm up well before climbing, use proper technique, and consider incorporating extensor training to balance finger flexor strength. Remember, take your time to avoid re-injury.", "citations": {"clueweb22-en0007-15-09796:7": 4.1157224779968935, "clueweb22-en0007-15-09796:8": 4.21494287001803, "clueweb22-en0015-71-10781:2": 2.7965166310234713, "clueweb22-en0015-71-10781:15": 3.873926302041363, "clueweb22-en0007-15-09796:9": 3.3644716147765705, "clueweb22-en0033-87-09727:6": 3.1815332056572894, "clueweb22-en0007-15-09796:3": 4.043991180731533, "clueweb22-en0026-20-01553:17": 2.7659946107744613}, "ptkb_provenance": []}], "references": {"clueweb22-en0007-15-09796:2": 4.698676757606471, "clueweb22-en0