{"metadata": {"team_id": "ucsc", "run_id": "UCSC-SIMRAG-ensemble", "run_type": "automatic"}, "turn_id": "3-1_8", "responses": [{"rank": 1, "text": "After a few weeks off, you can begin your return to climbing gradually. First, ensure your finger has a full, pain-free range of motion. You can start with gentle re-strengthening exercises like squeezing putty or a stress ball, finger flexions, and using a rice bucket. Massaging the flexor tendons and pulleys can also help prevent scar tissue and increase mobility.\n\nWhen you begin climbing again, it's advised to start with relatively easy routes that have big holds and good footholds, keeping your sessions short. You should avoid crimping, overhangs, and campus boards, as well as tweaky crimps and pockets for several months. Using prophylactic taping on your finger can provide support.\n\nGradually increase the intensity and duration of your climbing. A full return to activity is realistic in six to eight weeks for Grade 2/3 injuries, but a full functional return can take three to four months or more. Continue with stretching and strength-training exercises indefinitely as a preventive measure. The key is to listen to your body and not push through any pain to avoid re-injury.", "citations": {"clueweb22-en0017-96-08323:5": 4.846694643834049, "clueweb22-en0007-15-09796:11": 4.806830576346195, "clueweb22-en0030-70-18403:7": 4.4255974309299395, "clueweb22-en0017-96-08323:6": 4.208630978500275, "clueweb22-en0015-71-10781:14": 4.1619319045719445, "clueweb22-en0015-71-10781:13": 4.110009550557753, "clueweb22-en0015-71-10781:12": 3.967958238831471}, "ptkb_provenance": []}], "references": {"clueweb22-en0017-96-08323:5": 4.846694643834049, "clueweb22-en0007-15-09796:11": 4.806830576346195, "clueweb22-en0007-15-09796:12": 4.593972946236487, "clueweb22-en0015-71-10781:0": 4.531972145152596, "clueweb22-en0015-71-10781:1": 4.485295113556992, "clueweb22-en0005-83-07260:3": 4.458274781099912, "clueweb22-en0030-70-18403:7": 4.4255974