{"metadata": {"team_id": "ucsc", "run_id": "UCSC-SIMRAG-ensemble", "run_type": "automatic"}, "turn_id": "3-1_9", "responses": [{"rank": 1, "text": "Taping your finger for a pulley injury helps provide extra support to the injured pulley, keeping the tendons close to the bone and preventing further bowstringing. It's used during recovery to reduce load and can also serve as a psychological reminder of your injury.\n\nResearch indicates that H-taping offers the most substantial support for injured pulleys. Here's how to apply it:\n1.  Cut a piece of rigid athletic tape (like Leukoplast or Leukotape P) about 10cm long and 1.5cm wide.\n2.  Cut the tape lengthwise from both ends, leaving a 1cm bridge in the middle.\n3.  Place the tape on the injured finger with the bridge on the inside (palmar side), sitting on the crease of the joint at the upper end of the injured pulley (e.g., A2).\n4.  Flex your finger 60\u00b0 at the joint being taped.\n5.  Wrap the upper part of the tape around the finger to form a ring, then wrap the other part below the joint to form a second ring.\n\nIt's crucial to retape your finger between each climb or whenever you feel the tape loosening, as it stretches quickly. This method is particularly recommended for Grade 2 and 3 pulley injuries while climbing. Avoid \"buddy taping\" as it's not effective for pulley injuries.", "citations": {"clueweb22-en0033-87-09727:6": 4.949795189565178, "clueweb22-en0033-87-09727:7": 4.811006371792244, "clueweb22-en0033-87-09727:5": 4.718824885330974, "clueweb22-en0033-87-09727:11": 4.682055957782319, "clueweb22-en0033-87-09727:8": 4.660492735490169, "clueweb22-en0033-87-09727:10": 4.504191156953872, "clueweb22-en0033-87-09727:4": 4.358752550013795, "clueweb22-en0033-87-09727:9": 4.317007033855088, "clueweb22-en0033-87-09727:13": 4.073513063372311, "clueweb22-en0033-87-09727:14": 3.5937810246648905, "clueweb22-en0007-15-09796:8": 3.554478455907959, "clueweb22-en0007-15-09796:7": 3.552204043824732, "clueweb22-en00