{"metadata": {"team_id": "ucsc", "run_id": "UCSC-SIMRAG-ensemble", "run_type": "automatic"}, "turn_id": "3-1_10", "responses": [{"rank": 1, "text": "H-taping is a highly effective method for supporting injured finger pulleys, particularly when recovering from climbing injuries. Research by Doctor Sch\u00f6ffl and colleagues shows it's more effective than other methods like X or circular taping in keeping tendons close to the bone, especially when crimping. It has also been found to increase the crimping strength of injured fingers by an average of 13%.\n\nTo apply H-tape:\n1.  Cut a piece of rigid athletic tape (like Leukoplast) about 10cm long and 1.5cm wide.\n2.  Cut the tape in half lengthwise from both ends, leaving a 1cm bridge in the middle.\n3.  Place the tape on the injured finger with the bridge on the inside of the finger, sitting on the crease of the joint on the upper end of the injured pulley.\n4.  Flex your finger 60\u00b0 at the joint being taped.\n5.  Wrap the upper part of the tape in a ring, then wrap the other ring of tape below the joint.\n\nThis method is particularly useful for preventing reinjury and provides substantial support to your pulley in holding the tendons close to the bone, preventing bowstringing. Remember to retape between climbs or whenever the tape slackens, as its effectiveness decreases with stretching.", "citations": {"clueweb22-en0033-87-09727:9": 5.0, "clueweb22-en0033-87-09727:8": 4.568191565917278, "clueweb22-en0007-15-09796:10": 4.459742354617168, "clueweb22-en0033-87-09727:6": 4.313411891873826, "clueweb22-en0033-87-09727:5": 4.224080546830494, "clueweb22-en0033-87-09727:7": 4.221068227541676, "clueweb22-en0033-87-09727:14": 4.097960483733535, "clueweb22-en0007-15-09796:9": 4.029708734798239, "clueweb22-en0033-87-09727:13": 3.950317342032347, "clueweb22-en0033-87-09727:10": 3.5252281966022796}, "ptkb_provenance": []}], "references": {"clueweb22-en0033-87-09727:9": 5.0, "clueweb22-en0033-87-09727:8": 4.568191565917278, "