{"metadata": {"team_id": "ucsc", "run_id": "UCSC-SIMRAG-ensemble", "run_type": "automatic"}, "turn_id": "4-1_1", "responses": [{"rank": 1, "text": "It's great you want to prepare for a run! As a beginner, starting slowly and building your fitness gradually is key to avoiding injury and staying motivated.\n\nBefore you begin, consider getting a health check-up, especially if you haven't been very active. To prepare for your runs, ensure you're well-hydrated and have eaten a small meal with slow-releasing carbohydrates about two hours beforehand. Good options include a banana and yogurt, or a small bowl of pasta. Remember you have a peanut allergy, so avoid any food suggestions containing peanuts.\n\nInvest in comfortable running shoes, breathable socks, and lightweight clothing. For your first few runs, choose a relatively flat route close to home.\n\nA great way to start is with a walk/run strategy. You can begin by walking for 5-10 minutes at a brisk pace, then incorporate short running intervals, like jogging for 30 seconds followed by walking for 4:30 minutes, repeating for 15-20 minutes total. Gradually increase your running time and decrease walking time as your fitness improves. A 30-day run-walk challenge can help you build endurance to run for 30 minutes non-stop.\n\nAlways warm up with dynamic stretches like heel flicks or a brisk walk before you run, and allow for rest days. Don't feel pressured to run daily; starting with 2-3 runs a week is perfectly fine. Taking it slowly and consistently will help you build a strong running base and confidence.", "citations": {"clueweb22-en0026-69-09197:0": 3.7011354253470174, "clueweb22-en0009-29-16225:6": 3.363626425277754, "clueweb22-en0022-95-09628:3": 3.2377714107446938, "clueweb22-en0013-38-02830:9": 3.1535290337586694, "clueweb22-en0036-78-13542:8": 3.1520972997953227, "clueweb22-en0024-80-11518:2": 3.1463769242021216, "clueweb22-en0036-78-13542:9": 3.13243254879757, "clueweb22-en0002-44-17323:18": 3.126514224213