{"metadata": {"team_id": "ucsc", "run_id": "UCSC-SIMRAG-ensemble", "run_type": "automatic"}, "turn_id": "4-1_2", "responses": [{"rank": 1, "text": "Preparing for a 10-mile run is a fantastic goal, especially if you're not very confident in your fitness level! It's best to choose a training plan specifically designed for beginners, which will gradually ease you into the distance and help prevent injuries.\n\nThese plans typically range from 10 to 12 weeks, or even up to three months. Before starting, some beginner plans suggest you should be able to comfortably run or run/walk 3 miles. If you're not quite there yet, you might consider starting with a 15K (9.3 miles) beginner plan, which can be started if you can comfortably run 1.5 miles, and then transition to the 10-mile plan.\n\nA typical beginner 10-mile training plan will include:\n*   **Long Runs**: Gradually increasing mileage by 10-15% week over week.\n*   **Maintenance Runs**: Shorter runs (2-3 miles) to maintain weekly mileage and focus on form.\n*   **Cross-Training**: Essential for strengthening muscles, preventing injuries, and improving form. Activities like yoga, strength training, biking, or swimming are great options.\n*   **Rest Days**: Crucial for recovery, with most plans including two rest days per week.\n\nYou'll also need to prepare with the right gear, including long run fuel for runs over 60 minutes, a portable water bottle for hydration, and a foam roller for muscle recovery. Remember to experiment with fuel and hydration during training, not on race day. Mentally, breaking the 10 miles into smaller segments can help manage the distance.", "citations": {"clueweb22-en0019-07-06552:3": 4.805643733230914, "clueweb22-en0026-55-13129:15": 4.7163502631697, "clueweb22-en0004-89-17215:3": 4.64090955217368, "clueweb22-en0008-64-18271:11": 4.601962276038276, "clueweb22-en0008-64-18271:10": 4.535233654812815, "clueweb22-en0033-80-17520:2": 4.466468744711517, "clueweb22-en0026-55-13129:14": 4.3622548774