{"metadata": {"team_id": "ucsc", "run_id": "UCSC-SIMRAG-ensemble", "run_type": "automatic"}, "turn_id": "4-1_3", "responses": [{"rank": 1, "text": "Okay, preparing for a 10 km (6.2 miles) run is a fantastic goal, especially as you've mentioned not being very confident in your fitness level. For beginners, it's generally recommended to allow 8 to 12 weeks for training to build your endurance safely and effectively.\n\nHere are some key tips for your 10k preparation:\n*   **Start Gradually:** The most important thing is to build your distance slowly. Don't increase your total weekly distance by more than 10-15%. Beginning with 15-minute sessions, including walking breaks, is perfectly fine. As you get fitter, you can gradually reduce the walking sections.\n*   **Consistency is Key:** Aim to run at least three times per week. These runs should mostly be at an easy, conversational pace to reduce injury risk.\n*   **Build Long Runs:** Gradually work up to a long run that covers at least 7.5 km (75% of the race distance).\n*   **Rest and Recovery:** Factor in rest days between your runs to allow your muscles to repair and recover. Integrating cross-training, like swimming, can also be a great way to maintain cardiovascular fitness while giving your legs a break.\n*   **Base Building:** If you're new to running, focus on building a solid aerobic base before introducing any speed work, as increasing mileage and intensity simultaneously heightens injury risk.\n\nA beginner's 10k finish time typically ranges from 50 to 70 minutes, so focus on completing the distance comfortably. You can also look for structured training plans online or through running apps, which can guide you.", "citations": {"clueweb22-en0034-83-04863:5": 4.947825900577213, "clueweb22-en0034-83-04863:0": 4.6038849320518205, "clueweb22-en0007-91-03974:0": 4.5956302125871575, "clueweb22-en0004-91-11430:1": 4.544069188924231, "clueweb22-en0019-45-10567:5": 4.542549992870059, "clueweb22-en0008-57-08667:0": 4.36