{"metadata": {"team_id": "ucsc", "run_id": "UCSC-SIMRAG-ensemble", "run_type": "automatic"}, "turn_id": "4-2_2", "responses": [{"rank": 1, "text": "For your trip to New York, focusing on quiet museums and flea markets, here are some recommendations:\n\nFor a quieter museum experience, consider visiting **The Cloisters** (Passage 1), a stunning sister museum to the Metropolitan Museum of Art, located in Upper Manhattan. It's described as transporting you to a French castle, filled with tapestries and gardens, offering a peaceful escape. Another tip for avoiding crowds at popular museums like the Metropolitan Museum of Art or the Museum of Modern Art is to visit during their **late hours** (Passage 11). You can also explore the **free museums** (Passages 10, 15) available in the city, which might offer a more relaxed atmosphere. Staten Island also has museums that appeal to history and culture lovers (Passage 9).\n\nIf you enjoy browsing markets, you're in luck! New York has some great options. Check out the **Brooklyn Night Bazaar** (Passage 20) on Friday and Saturday nights for independently-made goods, combined with live music, food, and activities like karaoke and ping pong \u2013 perfect for young adults. You can also find **flea markets** (Passage 14) throughout the city. Additionally, **regular farmer's markets**, such as the one in Union Square (Passage 13) on Mondays, Wednesdays, Fridays, and Saturdays, are excellent for exploring and grabbing an affordable, fresh lunch.\n\nFor general travel tips, especially if you're a young adult on a budget, remember that many museums offer deals or free admission (Passages 10, 15). Public transportation like the bus and rail system is affordable for getting around (Passage 3). You can also find good deals on hotels by using platforms like Hotwire (Passage 17). Remember to factor in a 20% tip at restaurants (Passage 13).", "citations": {"clueweb22-en0011-88-06933:3": 4.684954226557784, "clueweb22-en0020-88-00965:2": 4.673