{"metadata": {"team_id": "ucsc", "run_id": "UCSC-SIMRAG-ensemble", "run_type": "automatic"}, "turn_id": "5-1_10", "responses": [{"rank": 1, "text": "Compared to regular sugar, which is linked to various lifestyle diseases like obesity, diabetes, and heart issues, other sweeteners offer different profiles.\n\nStevia and erythritol are generally considered among the healthiest options. Both have virtually no calories or carbohydrates and a glycemic index (GI) of 0, meaning they don't raise blood sugar levels. Stevia offers additional benefits like being anti-diabetic and anti-oxidant, while erythritol has antioxidant properties and can reduce fructose absorption. However, excessive intake of either can lead to some digestive discomfort or other minor side effects.\n\nXylitol is another good alternative, with nearly 40% fewer calories than table sugar and a GI of 13. It doesn't raise blood sugar or insulin levels, doesn't cause tooth decay, and can aid calcium absorption, though large amounts may cause digestive issues. Yacon syrup, with a GI of 1, is linked to weight loss and aids digestion due to its fiber and antioxidants.\n\nNatural sugars like honey, molasses, and maple syrup are considered less harmful than refined white sugar and offer some health benefits such as antioxidants and minerals. However, they are still calorie-rich and have higher glycemic indexes (Honey: 35-58, Molasses: 54, Maple Syrup: 54) compared to the sugar alcohols. Maple syrup specifically has similar calories and carbs to white sugar. Therefore, these should still be used sparingly, especially if you're managing blood sugar levels.", "citations": {"clueweb22-en0045-54-01249:7": 4.989710993663816, "clueweb22-en0009-63-03406:11": 4.305707113654759, "clueweb22-en0022-82-01437:10": 4.22613187436555, "clueweb22-en0026-66-11327:17": 4.140196271774888, "clueweb22-en0045-54-01249:6": 4.0540621925787725, "clueweb22-en0044-05-16388:9": 3.9519887803394758, "clueweb22-en0029-01-00630:2": 3.820278417346