{"metadata": {"team_id": "ucsc", "run_id": "UCSC-SIMRAG-ensemble", "run_type": "automatic"}, "turn_id": "5-2_5", "responses": [{"rank": 1, "text": "During pregnancy, you don't need to go on a special diet, but focusing on a healthy, balanced, and varied diet is crucial for both you and your baby. The idea of \"eating for two\" is a myth; your energy requirements are similar to normal in the first trimester, but you'll need a little extra\u2014around 200-350 calories per day in the second trimester and 350-500 in the third.\n\nPrioritize nutrient-dense, whole foods. Include plenty of fruits and vegetables, whole grains (like brown rice, whole-wheat bread), lean proteins (meat, fish, eggs, beans), and calcium-rich dairy products (milk, yogurt, cheese). Drink lots of water.\n\nLimit foods high in fat and sugar, such as sweets, cakes, and sugary drinks. It's vital to avoid certain foods due to potential harm: high-mercury fish (shark, swordfish, marlin), soft cheeses (brie, camembert, stilton), raw or undercooked seafood, meat, eggs, and poultry. Alcohol and cigarettes should also be eliminated. A folic acid supplement is also necessary. Importantly, do not diet or try to lose weight during pregnancy.", "citations": {"clueweb22-en0012-37-11383:13": 4.8691074110098524, "clueweb22-en0000-09-19416:0": 4.782035901325085, "clueweb22-en0014-61-10226:3": 4.605741450770641, "clueweb22-en0027-89-15500:0": 4.582117062088527, "clueweb22-en0022-69-17702:17": 4.496898559799794, "clueweb22-en0028-81-12071:2": 4.3961525930268115, "clueweb22-en0028-37-01996:1": 4.365334586965033, "clueweb22-en0001-10-19534:7": 4.342150637589872, "clueweb22-en0026-42-08361:1": 4.316358965051311, "clueweb22-en0008-09-13508:1": 4.310349410559556, "clueweb22-en0028-37-01996:2": 4.298391627799694, "clueweb22-en0035-01-11070:0": 4.29449458940887, "clueweb22-en0008-88-00414:0": 4.270805429469148, "clueweb22-en0038-78-08235:8": 4.265193128812239, "clueweb22-en0008-96-17009:15": 4.2391044064291865}, "ptkb_prove