{"metadata": {"team_id": "ucsc", "run_id": "UCSC-SIMRAG-ensemble", "run_type": "automatic"}, "turn_id": "5-2_6", "responses": [{"rank": 1, "text": "Yes, jam can be a beneficial part of your diet during pregnancy! You mentioned you enjoy jam for breakfast, and it's good news that it can offer several health benefits.\n\nJam, particularly when made from fully ripened fruits, retains many of the fruit's health benefits, including antioxidant power. Some research suggests that jam can help reduce the risk of neural tube defects, spina bifida, and anencephaly during fetal development (Passages 4, 8, 14, 20).\n\nCertain types of jam can also provide additional nutritional value. For instance, jams made with chia seeds can be a source of fiber, Omega-3 fatty acids, calcium, magnesium, and protein (Passages 1, 5). Additionally, ginger jam has been noted as potentially helpful for alleviating morning sickness (Passages 11, 18).\n\nThe pectin used as a gelling agent in jams is considered safe for consumption in small amounts during pregnancy by health authorities like the American Academy of Family Physicians (Passages 9, 10).\n\nAs with any food, moderation is key, especially since jams can be high in sugar. However, it can certainly be included as part of a balanced and varied diet, such as a small amount on whole wheat toast (Passage 17).", "citations": {"clueweb22-en0023-61-18698:10": 4.719669212600987, "clueweb22-en0031-32-15160:5": 4.402523542193338, "clueweb22-en0023-61-18698:9": 4.241283221386752, "clueweb22-en0031-32-15160:4": 4.1974163857812385, "clueweb22-en0008-26-06180:2": 4.015223331931345, "clueweb22-en0008-26-06180:3": 4.003319789522624, "clueweb22-en0018-95-05203:4": 3.9849719101304686, "clueweb22-en0009-11-11998:4": 3.7428804187287925, "clueweb22-en0008-88-00414:7": 3.583768173964053, "clueweb22-en0018-95-05203:3": 3.4635998236104584, "clueweb22-en0009-11-11998:3": 3.4574625340591423}, "ptkb_provenance": []}], "references": {"clueweb22-en0023-61-18698:10": 4