{"metadata": {"team_id": "ucsc", "run_id": "UCSC-SIMRAG-ensemble", "run_type": "automatic"}, "turn_id": "5-2_8", "responses": [{"rank": 1, "text": "Yes, drinking alcohol can impact your breast milk supply and your baby's milk intake. Studies show that alcohol can reduce milk production and hinder the milk ejection reflex (let-down) by affecting hormones like oxytocin. This means that even small amounts of alcohol can disrupt the balance of milk-producing hormones.\n\nContrary to old beliefs that alcohol, like beer, increases milk supply, modern research indicates it's more likely to lower it. Babies tend to consume less milk when there's alcohol present, even if they nurse more frequently. For instance, some studies show babies may drink 20-23% less milk in the hours after their mother consumes alcohol.\n\nWhile occasional, moderate drinking might not significantly affect a robust milk supply, chronic or substantial alcohol consumption can compromise your overall milk supply and your baby's growth. Excessive drinking can lead to reduced milk intake for the baby, potentially resulting in slow weight gain or failure to thrive.", "citations": {"clueweb22-en0031-57-16983:0": 4.8352903726784096, "clueweb22-en0046-33-11913:13": 4.676034556470619, "clueweb22-en0046-33-11913:10": 4.535739162329065, "clueweb22-en0046-33-11913:9": 4.508753187195365, "clueweb22-en0002-39-11926:5": 4.451961842290557, "clueweb22-en0004-28-10513:8": 4.403231532618218, "clueweb22-en0005-68-03159:4": 4.397960542129285}, "ptkb_provenance": []}], "references": {"clueweb22-en0031-57-16983:0": 4.8352903726784096, "clueweb22-en0042-99-06708:12": 4.758226774368919, "clueweb22-en0046-33-11913:13": 4.676034556470619, "clueweb22-en0034-22-10973:1": 4.5938692447543685, "clueweb22-en0004-28-10513:5": 4.565961714806494, "clueweb22-en0039-46-12412:9": 4.550000883587961, "clueweb22-en0046-33-11913:10": 4.535739162329065, "clueweb22-en0004-28-10513:4": 4.510399598539913, "clueweb22-en0033-22-04249:4": 4.509736237