{"metadata": {"team_id": "ucsc", "run_id": "UCSC-SIMRAG-ensemble", "run_type": "automatic"}, "turn_id": "5-2_10", "responses": [{"rank": 1, "text": "It's completely normal for babies to cry, and it's their main way of communicating their needs. While it might be difficult to hear, it's an expected and healthy part of infancy.\n\nNewborns typically cry for an average of 1 to 3 hours a day, though some may cry up to 5 hours within a 24-hour period. This crying is usually spaced out. You might notice that crying often increases around 2 to 3 weeks of age and tends to peak at about 6 to 8 weeks. Many parents find their baby's fussiness is more common in the late afternoon or early evening.\n\nThe good news is that this period of intense crying usually doesn't last forever. Crying gradually decreases after the first few weeks, and by around 3 months of age, babies typically cry for about an hour a day. So, while your baby will cry, it won't be constant, and you'll learn to understand their different cries as they grow. Babies cry to communicate various needs like hunger, discomfort, or a need for attention.", "citations": {"clueweb22-en0038-57-04444:0": 4.915867562036796, "clueweb22-en0029-92-05890:2": 4.779273186673189, "clueweb22-en0043-40-01751:2": 4.641756282387526, "clueweb22-en0022-43-13342:10": 4.606087096618472, "clueweb22-en0013-59-01598:13": 4.542583226478332, "clueweb22-en0001-28-08060:7": 4.490899405001291, "clueweb22-en0029-92-05890:1": 4.436716986004277, "clueweb22-en0000-45-00661:0": 4.381254851955759, "clueweb22-en0016-36-07010:1": 4.267146309622725, "clueweb22-en0045-47-16801:1": 4.236989417808678, "clueweb22-en0011-17-01403:7": 4.218105087556219, "clueweb22-en0026-56-09659:2": 4.216874872581233, "clueweb22-en0001-87-12954:4": 4.175176695429883, "clueweb22-en0026-56-09659:1": 4.139409733387681, "clueweb22-en0015-34-03797:3": 4.130902640283855, "clueweb22-en0043-40-01751:1": 4.101662435424542, "clueweb22-en0013-59-01598:12": 4.0747403104900135, "clueweb22