{"metadata": {"team_id": "ucsc", "run_id": "UCSC-SIMRAG-ensemble", "run_type": "automatic"}, "turn_id": "6-1_6", "responses": [{"rank": 1, "text": "The main difference between fully ablative CO2 laser treatment and fractional CO2 laser treatment lies in how much skin is targeted and removed.\n\n**Fully Ablative CO2 Lasers** completely remove the entire top layer of skin in the treated area, heating deeper layers to stimulate collagen production. This intense procedure usually requires general anesthesia, meaning you'll be completely asleep. Recovery time is significant, typically a minimum of two weeks, during which your skin will be swollen, scabbed, and raw. One treatment session is often sufficient for desired results. These lasers are generally recommended only for fair or light skin tones due to the risk of pigmentation issues.\n\n**Fractional CO2 Lasers**, on the other hand, deliver a partial beam of light that targets only a fraction of the skin in a pixelated pattern, leaving surrounding skin untouched. This makes the treatment considerably less aggressive. It usually requires only local anesthesia and possibly an oral sedative, and the downtime is significantly shorter, often just a matter of days. However, you typically need a series of treatments to achieve the best results. Fractional CO2 is also a safer option for a wider range of skin tones, including medium to deep, and is effective for mild to moderate signs of aging and hyperpigmentation.", "citations": {"clueweb22-en0004-01-09924:10": 5.0, "clueweb22-en0004-01-09924:12": 4.477614499342009, "clueweb22-en0004-01-09924:11": 4.470218113323416, "clueweb22-en0004-01-09924:8": 3.9359123735775436, "clueweb22-en0004-01-09924:13": 3.865835330063359, "clueweb22-en0022-44-12230:2": 3.4102973040575524}, "ptkb_provenance": []}], "references": {"clueweb22-en0004-01-09924:10": 5.0, "clueweb22-en0004-01-09924:9": 4.555022107690592, "clueweb22-en0004-01-09924:12": 4.477614499342009, "clueweb22-en0004-01-09924:11": 4