{"metadata": {"team_id": "ucsc", "run_id": "UCSC-SIMRAG-ensemble", "run_type": "automatic"}, "turn_id": "7-1_3", "responses": [{"rank": 1, "text": "Baking bread in a conventional oven allows for great control and a satisfying result. To start, preheat your oven thoroughly, often for at least 20 minutes, or even up to an hour for artisan loaves, ensuring your baking surface (like a baking stone or preheated sheet pan) is piping hot. Many recipes suggest an initial high temperature, such as 250\u00b0C (480\u00b0F) or 500\u00b0F, which you then reduce after placing the bread in the oven.\n\nCreating steam in the oven is crucial for a crisp, chewy crust and good \"oven spring.\" You can achieve this by placing an oven-safe bowl of boiling water on the bottom rack, or by throwing ice cubes into a preheated cast iron skillet on the bottom rack right after you put the bread in. Some methods also suggest spraying the oven walls with water.\n\nBefore baking, score the top of your dough with a sharp knife or lame, about 0.4 to 2 cm deep, to control expansion. Bake for the time specified in your recipe, typically 30-60 minutes, often reducing the temperature after the first 10-15 minutes. The bread is done when the internal temperature reaches 190\u00b0F-205\u00b0F (88\u00b0C-96\u00b0C) and the bottom sounds hollow when tapped. Always cool the bread completely on a wire rack before slicing to allow the internal structure to set.", "citations": {"clueweb22-en0026-86-17371:2": 5.0, "clueweb22-en0036-42-10533:7": 4.401676831915257, "clueweb22-en0031-22-16887:18": 4.29821807942446, "clueweb22-en0041-43-00646:13": 4.2921965004891165, "clueweb22-en0013-44-14322:9": 4.131724656393265, "clueweb22-en0026-82-14253:19": 4.069702310588419, "clueweb22-en0036-15-04794:14": 4.033082306240981, "clueweb22-en0022-04-13871:4": 3.971414254862502, "clueweb22-en0039-87-14873:12": 3.9682889044253478, "clueweb22-en0000-12-01744:9": 3.9593091945299084}, "ptkb_provenance": []}], "references": {"clueweb