{"metadata": {"team_id": "ucsc", "run_id": "UCSC-SIMRAG-ensemble", "run_type": "automatic"}, "turn_id": "8-1_7", "responses": [{"rank": 1, "text": "It's great that you're looking for ways to protect your hyperfocus! When you're in the zone, external interruptions like emails and meetings can be particularly jarring.\n\nFor emails, a key strategy is to manage when and how you check them. Instead of letting notifications pull you away, set specific times during the day to check and respond to emails \u2013 perhaps just a few times a day, like first thing in the morning, before lunch, and at the end of the day. You can also turn off audible and visual alerts for incoming messages or even set your email software to only receive messages at those designated times. If something truly urgent comes up, consider setting up an autoresponder that directs people to reach you by phone or in person for immediate needs. Another tip is to jot down any distracting thoughts or ideas that pop up while you're focused, so you can address them later without losing your flow.\n\nFor meetings, one effective approach is to compartmentalize your workweek. You could try dedicating specific days or blocks of time solely for meetings, leaving other days as interruption-free as possible for your deep, hyperfocused work. Before attending a meeting, always ensure there's a clear agenda, and don't hesitate to question recurring meetings or attendance lists if they don't seem essential for you. Using electronic reminders for appointments can help you transition smoothly without being caught off guard.", "citations": {"clueweb22-en0025-75-16053:1": 4.902090135410124, "clueweb22-en0038-88-03812:8": 4.65589214793124, "clueweb22-en0038-88-03812:7": 4.422559989786471, "clueweb22-en0025-75-16053:2": 4.399125234152335, "clueweb22-en0030-54-15087:16": 4.385109406200548, "clueweb22-en0041-12-03522:7": 4.104097466771606, "clueweb22-en0006-62-03234:3": 4.063873210430666, "clueweb22-en0030-04-10293:2": 4.032676