{"metadata": {"team_id": "ucsc", "run_id": "UCSC-SIMRAG-ensemble", "run_type": "automatic"}, "turn_id": "8-1_8", "responses": [{"rank": 1, "text": "To manage meetings without disrupting your hyperfocus, consider these strategies:\n\nOne effective approach is to compartmentalize your workweek by designating specific \"meeting days\" (e.g., Mondays and Fridays). This allows you to keep other days as interruption-free as possible for deep work and hyperfocus (Passage 1, 4). If full meeting-free days aren't feasible, try to schedule essential meetings during times when your energy is typically lower, such as late afternoon, to protect your peak focus periods (Passage 19).\n\nYou can also use a calendar tool to plan out your week, blocking out uninterrupted time for your most important tasks and scheduling meetings around those blocks (Passage 6). It's helpful to communicate your availability to colleagues and your boss, setting expectations about when you're available for meetings versus when you're in deep work (Passage 5).\n\nTo minimize disruptions, suggest replacing some status meetings with project management software or other communication channels (Passage 5). For unavoidable meetings, prepare beforehand by sharing an agenda so everyone knows what to expect (Passage 3, 18). If you know a meeting is approaching while you're hyperfocused, you can set a timer a bit before it starts to gently transition out of your focus mode (Passage 7). During the meeting, taking notes can help you stay engaged (Passage 14, 15).", "citations": {"clueweb22-en0006-62-03234:3": 4.878143153594322, "clueweb22-en0016-95-16259:4": 4.623694394158965, "clueweb22-en0012-26-11087:1": 4.329561832185429, "clueweb22-en0044-27-14365:9": 4.3075451842044465, "clueweb22-en0006-19-19181:13": 4.082572560937613, "clueweb22-en0013-60-10409:8": 4.078409966387097, "clueweb22-en0019-85-03855:11": 3.7681699923159506, "clueweb22-en0038-88-03812:10": 3.719534315296579, "clueweb22-en0032-71-14252:2": 3.6396499