{"metadata": {"team_id": "ucsc", "run_id": "UCSC-SIMRAG-ensemble", "run_type": "automatic"}, "turn_id": "8-1_9", "responses": [{"rank": 1, "text": "Yes, meetings can definitely be a significant distraction, especially when you're in a state of hyperfocus. The key is to proactively manage your schedule and the meetings themselves to protect your deep work periods.\n\nOne effective strategy is to compartmentalize your workweek. You could designate specific days or large blocks of time solely for meetings, accepting that these might not be your most productive work days. Conversely, you would then have other days or blocks of time that are as interruption-free as possible for your focused work and hyperfocus sessions. This means blocking out chunks of time for your deep work and not automatically making yourself available for meetings during those periods.\n\nAdditionally, you can be more proactive about the meetings themselves. Always try to attend meetings with an agenda, and don't hesitate to question recurring meetings or challenge the attendance list if you feel they are unnecessary for you. This can help reduce the overall number of meetings that might disrupt your flow.\n\nFinally, consider engineering your environment to minimize general distractions. This might involve closing your office door or working in a designated quiet space when you need to enter a hyperfocused state, which can help prevent unexpected interruptions that might lead to impromptu discussions or meetings.", "citations": {"clueweb22-en0041-12-03522:7": 4.732868330599662, "clueweb22-en0006-62-03234:3": 4.715937395100582, "clueweb22-en0030-54-15087:16": 3.930573787786471, "clueweb22-en0007-81-09685:15": 3.809737807891157}, "ptkb_provenance": []}], "references": {"clueweb22-en0041-12-03522:7": 4.732868330599662, "clueweb22-en0006-62-03234:3": 4.715937395100582, "clueweb22-en0006-80-11686:3": 4.39966575906384, "clueweb22-en0013-60-10409:8": 4.375884765334358, "clueweb22-en0022-33-08001:9": 4.24