{"metadata": {"team_id": "ucsc", "run_id": "UCSC-SIMRAG-ensemble", "run_type": "automatic"}, "turn_id": "8-1_10", "responses": [{"rank": 1, "text": "To help you reset your focus before and after meetings, you can implement several strategies:\n\n**Before a meeting:**\n*   **Create a buffer:** Schedule \"white space\" for 15 minutes before the meeting to prepare mentally. This can involve clearing your head, making notes, or getting into a good headspace for listening.\n*   **Short mindfulness/breathing exercises:** Take a 60-second time-out, or use the minutes before a meeting for a short meditation or deep breathing exercises to find your focus and calm your mind.\n\n**After a meeting:**\n*   **Micro-breaks and physical movement:** Immediately after a meeting, step away from your desk. Shake out your body, take deep breaths, stretch, rest your eyes, or even do a few jumping jacks. A physical change of location, like walking to get a glass of water, can help reset your mental energy.\n*   **Buffer time for debriefing:** Use the \"white space\" you scheduled to catch your breath, reflect on the meeting, and jot down any action steps or deadlines.\n*   **Short meditations:** Similar to before, a brief meditation can help reboot your energy and re-center your focus after the meeting has ended.\n\nThese strategies create a psychological buffer, helping your brain transition and reduce the \"attention residue\" that can make it hard to get back to focused work.", "citations": {"clueweb22-en0023-69-08765:9": 5.0, "clueweb22-en0041-78-03980:4": 4.145071974324476, "clueweb22-en0017-46-05051:7": 4.124042257626035, "clueweb22-en0022-70-00626:6": 4.063980898328784, "clueweb22-en0021-80-10230:3": 4.053966105663546, "clueweb22-en0043-07-14302:25": 3.941088976377298, "clueweb22-en0017-46-05051:6": 3.937597522382758, "clueweb22-en0009-21-04763:6": 3.6975501250107135, "clueweb22-en0043-07-14302:24": 3.661343009704678, "clueweb22-en0022-70-00626:5": 3.64820636744188, "clueweb22-en00