{"metadata": {"team_id": "ucsc", "run_id": "UCSC-SIMRAG-ensemble", "run_type": "automatic"}, "turn_id": "9-2_2", "responses": [{"rank": 1, "text": "To fix your bike chain yourself, first, you'll need to get your bike in a stable position. You can turn it upside down, resting it on the seat and handlebars, or use a bike stand if you have one (10, 11, 14).\n\nIf the chain has simply come off, you can often reattach it by hooking it onto the rear sprocket, then the bottom of the front sprocket, and carefully pedaling backward until it catches (3, 16).\n\nIf your chain keeps coming off, especially when shifting to the \"extreme gears,\" the issue is likely with your derailleur's limit screws (1, 2, 6). These screws, labeled \"H\" (High) and \"L\" (Low), control how far the derailleur can move. If the chain falls off the outside, turn the \"H\" screw clockwise. If it falls towards the wheel or between the chainring and seat tube, turn the \"L\" screw clockwise (1, 6). Adjust them until the derailleur lines up in the middle of the gear (1).\n\nAlso, inspect your chain, derailleurs, and cassette for any bent, broken, or worn-out parts, as these can cause the chain to slip (1, 5, 11, 14). If the chain is worn, it might need replacing, which requires a universal chain tool (1, 4, 5). Lubricating your chain regularly can also prevent future issues (2).", "citations": {"clueweb22-en0020-44-10600:19": 4.462269390320071, "clueweb22-en0000-80-11057:11": 4.208391377431871, "clueweb22-en0019-70-04653:6": 4.181073554265436, "clueweb22-en0022-62-09551:2": 4.1449775926992825, "clueweb22-en0019-70-04653:10": 4.117307872129536, "clueweb22-en0020-44-10600:18": 4.1022433280270985, "clueweb22-en0029-48-06882:11": 3.963796260143929, "clueweb22-en0020-44-10600:0": 3.958138478232039, "clueweb22-en0020-44-10600:1": 3.9308608349338967, "clueweb22-en0019-70-04653:5": 3.891453341753257}, "ptkb_provenance": []}], "references": {"clueweb22-en0020-44-10600:19": 4.462269390320071, "clueweb22-en0000-8