{"metadata": {"team_id": "ucsc", "run_id": "UCSC-SIMRAG-ensemble", "run_type": "automatic"}, "turn_id": "9-2_7", "responses": [{"rank": 1, "text": "You're right, there's a way to determine the ideal chain length without any math calculations, often called the \"largest cog and largest chainring method.\"\n\nTo do this:\n1.  Shift your bike to the largest front chainring and the largest rear cog.\n2.  Take your new chain and wrap it around these two largest gears, making sure to *bypass* the rear derailleur completely.\n3.  Bring the two ends of the chain together. Find the closest rivet where these two ends could be joined.\n4.  Once you've found that point, you'll need to add two more full links (or one outer and one inner link) to that length. This will be your cutting point for the new chain.\n\nThis method is generally considered the easiest way to determine the correct chain length.", "citations": {"clueweb22-en0009-91-00148:5": 4.260424838773824, "clueweb22-en0009-91-00148:2": 4.111863914449346}, "ptkb_provenance": []}], "references": {"clueweb22-en0009-91-00148:6": 4.54579233048959, "clueweb22-en0020-85-09566:14": 4.439406018232186, "clueweb22-en0020-85-09566:15": 4.315225506868148, "clueweb22-en0009-91-00148:5": 4.260424838773824, "clueweb22-en0020-85-09566:10": 4.183287149897452, "clueweb22-en0009-91-00148:2": 4.111863914449346, "clueweb22-en0038-37-00729:12": 3.852839256041537, "clueweb22-en0038-37-00729:13": 3.7764396034104104, "clueweb22-en0033-45-01084:12": 3.7583128393344065, "clueweb22-en0045-61-12659:12": 3.654849386999507, "clueweb22-en0024-98-15927:4": 3.5842455271832723, "clueweb22-en0009-91-00148:3": 3.5675402210266594, "clueweb22-en0024-98-15927:3": 3.564102216871871, "clueweb22-en0009-91-00148:1": 3.562546945622131, "clueweb22-en0009-91-00148:0": 3.5292267948431055, "clueweb22-en0020-85-09566:9": 3.5281157859745664, "clueweb22-en0045-61-12659:13": 3.482860038068958, "clueweb22-en0033-45-01084:13": 3.4387179000482013, "clueweb22-en0020-85-09566: